    "q-210763": {
      "dom_id": "step-q210763",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-|5x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
